Let $\mathcal A$ be a finite alphabet with $A\geq2$ letters. Each of the two constructions below gives an integer width $w\geq1$ with $w=O(\log(2A))$, an absolute constant $a>0$, and a map $c:\mathcal A\to\{0,1\}^w$ such that, simultaneously for every integer $L\geq0$ and all $x,y\in\mathcal A^L$, $$\label{p2:eq:log-code-deficit}
 a w r_L(x,y)\leq r_{wL}(c(x),c(y))\leq w r_L(x,y).$$ Here $c$ extends to words by concatenation. For arbitrary finite words, $$\label{p2:eq:log-code-upper}
 \operatorname{ED}(c(x),c(y))\leq w\operatorname{ED}(x,y).$$ The code and its width depend only on $\mathcal A$, not on $L$.

hypotheses.statement:
Let $R,L\geq1$ and $t\geq2$ be integers, and let $\Gamma_1,\ldots,\Gamma_t$ be pairwise disjoint alphabets. Each of two words consists of $R$ rows, each containing in order a block in $\Gamma_i^L$ for $i=1,\ldots,t$. Thus every matched pair preserves its child index. The common length is $N=RtL$. Fix an increasing matching with $E$ unmatched positions on each side. Suppose every nonsplit source row loses at least $AtL$ source positions, where $0\leq A\leq1$; rows with no matches are included in this hypothesis. Then $$\label{pre:row-bound}
 E\geq A\left(N-\frac{tE}{t-1}\right),\qquad
 E\geq\frac{AN}{1+tA/(t-1)}.$$

For every $0\leq\ell\leq h$, every $v\in G^{r_\ell}$, and every $c\in C_\ell$, $$\label{mask:cheap-subgroup}
 \operatorname{ED}(S_\ell(v),S_\ell(v+c))\leq\frac{2\ell L_\ell}{R}.$$ At the top level, for every leaf $i$ and $1\leq a\leq M/2$, $$\label{mask:cheap-leaf}
 \operatorname{ED}(S_h(v),S_h(v+e_i(a/M)))\leq\frac{2aL_h}{Mr_h}.$$ Both estimates hold at every base state $v$.

For every $0\leq\ell\leq h$, $v\in G^{r_\ell}$, and $\theta\in G$, $$\label{mask:separation-bound}
 r_{L_\ell}(S_\ell(v),S_\ell(v+\theta\mathbf1))
 \geq\frac{2^{-\ell}}4L_\ell D_\ell(\theta).$$

Let $H\leq V$, let $w=(1/2,\ldots,1/2)\in V$, and suppose $B>0$ satisfies $$\label{pre:branching-premise}
 \chi_\alpha|_H=1,\quad \chi_\alpha(w)\ne1
 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad T(\alpha)\geq B.$$ For every $F:V\to\ell_1$ there are nonnegative weights $\omega_\alpha$ with $$\label{pre:fourier}
 \Delta_F(a)=\sum_\alpha\omega_\alpha|1-\chi_\alpha(a)|^2
 \qquad(a\in V).$$ Writing $H^\perp=\{\alpha:\chi_\alpha|_H=1\}$, these weights satisfy $$\begin{aligned}
 \sum_{\alpha\notin H^\perp}\omega_\alpha
   &=\tfrac12\mathbb E_{u\in H}\Delta_F(u),\label{pre:off-mass}\\
 4\sum_\alpha\omega_\alpha T(\alpha)
   &\leq\sum_{i=1}^r\sum_{a\in\mathcal D_M}
        \frac Ma\Delta_F((a/M)e_i).\label{pre:weighted-mass}
\end{aligned}$$ Consequently $$\label{pre:displacement}
 \Delta_F(w)\leq2\mathbb E_{u\in H}\Delta_F(u)
 +\frac1B\sum_{i=1}^r\sum_{a\in\mathcal D_M}
        \frac Ma\Delta_F((a/M)e_i).$$

Let $\mathcal A$ be a finite alphabet with $A\geq2$ letters. Each of the two constructions below gives an integer width $w\geq1$ with $w=O(\log(2A))$, an absolute constant $a>0$, and a map $c:\mathcal A\to\{0,1\}^w$ such that, simultaneously for every integer $L\geq0$ and all $x,y\in\mathcal A^L$, $$\label{p2:eq:log-code-deficit}
 a w r_L(x,y)\leq r_{wL}(c(x),c(y))\leq w r_L(x,y).$$ Here $c$ extends to words by concatenation. For arbitrary finite words, $$\label{p2:eq:log-code-upper}
 \operatorname{ED}(c(x),c(y))\leq w\operatorname{ED}(x,y).$$ The code and its width depend only on $\mathcal A$, not on $L$.

For every node $a$ at height $0\le\ell\le h$, every $v\in G$, and every $\theta,z\in L^{-1}\mathbb Z/\mathbb Z$, $$\label{eq:exc-word-separation}
 r_{N_\ell}\bigl(W_a(v,\theta),W_a(v,\theta+z)\bigr)
 \ge N_\ell 3^{-\ell}\rho_\ell(z).$$

Let $\Delta(x,y)$ be the minimum number of insertions and deletions when substitutions are forbidden. For all finite words, $$\Delta(x,y)=|x|+|y|-2\operatorname{LCS}(x,y),\qquad
 \operatorname{ED}(x,y)\le\Delta(x,y)\le2\operatorname{ED}(x,y),$$ and $$\label{upper:unequal-comparison}
 q(x,y)\le\operatorname{ED}(x,y)\le2q(x,y).$$ In particular, for $|x|=|y|=n$, $$\label{upper:deficit-comparison}
 r_n(x,y)=\tfrac12\Delta(x,y),\qquad
 r_n(x,y)\le\operatorname{ED}(x,y)\le2r_n(x,y).$$

For equal-length words $u,v$ of length $m$ and any common suffix $z$, $$\label{upper:common-suffix}
 r_{m+|z|}(uz,vz)=r_m(u,v).$$ If $\ast$ occurs in neither $x$ nor $y$, and $N\ge\max(|x|,|y|)$, then, writing $\widehat x=x\ast^{N-|x|}$ and $\widehat y=y\ast^{N-|y|}$, $$\label{upper:fresh-padding}
 \operatorname{LCS}(\widehat x,\widehat y)
 =\operatorname{LCS}(x,y)+N-\max(|x|,|y|),\qquad
 r_N(\widehat x,\widehat y)=q(x,y).$$ The second identity includes empty inputs.

Let $X$ be finite and $\rho(u,v)=\|f(u)-f(v)\|_1$ for a map $f:X\to\ell_1$ into real sequence space. For every $\alpha>0$ there is a probability distribution with finite support on partitions $\pi$ of $X$ such that $$\label{upper:collision}
 \Pr[\pi(u)=\pi(v)]=e^{-\alpha\rho(u,v)}.$$ Here $\pi(u)$ denotes the part containing $u$. For a finite list $L$ in $X$, let $H_L^\pi$ be its count vector on the parts of $\pi$, with repetitions counted. Then $$\label{upper:histogram-map}
 \mathcal H_\alpha(L)=\bigoplus_\pi \Pr[\pi]\,H_L^\pi$$ is a deterministic finite-dimensional map whose distance equals $\mathbb E\|H_L^\pi-H_{L'}^\pi\|_1$.

Let $s\ge1$ be an integer and let $L,L'$ be lists of size $s$ in the set of Lemma <a href="#upper:partitions" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="upper:partitions">19</a>, and set $T=\mathbb E\|H_L^\pi-H_{L'}^\pi\|_1$. If every cross-pair has $\rho$-distance at least $a$, then $$\label{upper:histogram-lower}
 T\ge2s(1-s^2e^{-\alpha a}).$$ If a pairing of list occurrences leaves $t$ occurrences unpaired on each side, then $$\label{upper:coupling}
 T\le2t+2\sum_{(u,v)\ {\rm paired}}(1-e^{-\alpha\rho(u,v)})
   \le2t+2\alpha\sum_{(u,v)\ {\rm paired}}\rho(u,v).$$ Writing $P_L^\pi=H_L^\pi/s$ and $\operatorname{TV}(P,Q)=\tfrac12\|P-Q\|_1$, the exact normalizations are $$\label{upper:normalizations}
 \|H_L^\pi-H_{L'}^\pi\|_1
 =s\|P_L^\pi-P_{L'}^\pi\|_1
 =2s\,\operatorname{TV}(P_L^\pi,P_{L'}^\pi).$$

